\section{Database files}{

With \code{path}, the views, the \code{rcs} tables and the tables you create live in
a DuckDB file, created when missing. A file \code{work.duckdb} is attached as
the database \code{work}, so no dataset may have the first part \code{work}. The
file must lie outside every local location of the store, which a sync
would carry it to.

Calling \code{store_duckdb(st, path)} again on the same file refreshes it:
views are replaced to show the versions in \code{st}'s catalog, views of
deleted datasets are dropped, secrets are recreated, and your own tables
and views are left untouched (a table or a view not made by
\code{store_duckdb()} with a view's name aborts). \code{store_duckdb(path = )}
without \code{st} reconnects to the store recorded in \code{rcs.store}, with default
credentials, and refreshes the same way.

A plain \code{DBI::dbConnect(duckdb::duckdb(dbdir = path), bigint = "integer64")} also opens the file: your tables work, and native views
work while their files exist, but they show the versions of the last
refresh (a later write may have deleted those files, unless the store
keeps history), views on Google Cloud Storage need a secret first, and
views in arrow mode do not work until \code{store_duckdb()} is called again.
\code{\link[=store_tables]{store_tables()}} with \code{st} says which views are not current.
